  "name": "the war chest",
  "description": "Chess with a war chest. Every capture pays coins into the taker's purse: one for a pawn, two for a knight or a bishop, three for a rook, five for a queen. Instead of moving, a player may spend the whole turn buying a pawn for two coins, or a knight or a bishop for five, and set it on any empty square of their own back rank. So every exchange is priced twice, in material and in coins, and the back rank, crowded at the start, becomes a recruiting ground as it empties. The bounties sit below the prices on purpose: taking a knight pays two coins toward a five-coin knight, so the side that wins material can rebuild sooner, but no capture pays for what it took. A middle game tends to refill rather than thin out. An attack has to land before the defender buys fresh pieces behind its king, and every turn spent buying is a move the opponent gets to use. Both players start with two coins, enough for one pawn, and a purse holds at most 99.",
